  "full_response": "### **Graph-Based Data Modeling**\nGraph-based data modeling is a way of representing data as **nodes (entities)**, **edges (relationships)**, and **properties (attributes)**. Unlike traditional relational or document databases, graph databases focus on the **connections** between data points rather than just the data itself.\n\n#### **Key Concepts:**\n1. **Nodes** – Represent entities (e.g., people, products, places).\n2. **Edges** – Represent relationships between nodes (e.g., \"knows,\" \"bought,\" \"located in\").\n3. **Properties** – Key-value pairs attached to nodes or edges (e.g., `name: \"Alice\"`, `since: 2020`).\n4. **Labels** – Categories for nodes (e.g., `Person`, `Product`).\n\n#### **Example:**\n- **Nodes:** `User (Alice)`, `Product (Laptop)`\n- **Edge:** `Alice -[BOUGHT]-> Laptop` (with properties like `date: \"2023-10-01\"`)\n\n---\n\n### **When to Use a Graph Database**\nGraph databases excel in scenarios where **relationships are as important as the data itself**. Here are key use cases:\n\n#### **1. Highly Connected Data**\n- **Social Networks** (friendships, recommendations)\n- **Fraud Detection** (unusual transaction patterns)\n- **Recommendation Engines** (e.g., \"People who bought X also bought Y\")\n\n#### **2. Complex Queries Involving Relationships**\n- **Pathfinding** (e.g., shortest route in logistics)\n- **Hierarchical Data** (e.g., organizational charts, taxonomy)\n- **Dependency Analysis** (e.g., IT infrastructure, supply chains)\n\n#### **3. Real-Time Traversal of Relationships**\n- **Knowledge Graphs** (e.g., Wikipedia, semantic search)\n- **Network & IT Operations** (e.g., tracking dependencies in microservices)\n\n#### **4. When Joins Become Too Expensive**\n- In relational databases, complex joins slow down queries.\n- Graph databases **natively traverse relationships** without expensive joins.\n\n#### **5. Dynamic Schemas & Evolving Data**\n- Unlike rigid relational schemas, graph databases allow flexible relationships.\n\n---\n\n### **When NOT to Use a Graph Database**\n- **Simple, tabular data** (relational databases are better).\n- **High-write, low-relationship workloads** (e.g., logging",
